## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of low‑level memory management, freelists, and fixed-size allocators.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size blocks and simple freelist
- In-block metadata for next pointer
- Alignment guarantees and fragmentation considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// JavaScript-simulated fixed-size allocator on an ArrayBuffer
const POOL_SIZE = 64 * 1024;
const BLOCK_SIZE = 64;
const pool = new ArrayBuffer(POOL_SIZE);
const nextFree = new Int32Array(pool);
let head = 0;
function init_pool(){ const blocks = POOL_SIZE / BLOCK_SIZE; for(let i=0;i<blocks-1;i++){ nextFree[i] = i+1; } nextFree[blocks-1] = -1; head = 0; }
function my_malloc(){ if(head === -1) return null; const idx = head; head = nextFree[idx]; return new DataView(pool, idx*BLOCK_SIZE, BLOCK_SIZE); }
function my_free(ptr){ // ptr is DataView; compute index and push back to head
  const idx = Math.floor(ptr.byteOffset / BLOCK_SIZE);
  nextFree[idx] = head; head = idx;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to variable sizes? trade-offs?
- What are the impacts of contention in multi-thread setups?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of memory hierarchy and data locality by forcing cache-friendly tiling in a simple kernel.

## Key Concepts
- Tile size vs cache capacity; 2D blocking; stride-1 vs stride-N access patterns; bounds handling.
- Prefetching to hide memory latency; ensure no aliasing via separate buffers.

## Code Example
```c
void transpose_tiled(const float* A, float* B, int N) {
  const int Bsz = 32;
  for (int br = 0; br < N; br += Bsz) {
    for (int bc = 0; bc < N; bc += Bsz) {
      for (int i = 0; i < Bsz; ++i) {
        int r = br + i;
        if (r >= N) break;
        for (int j = 0; j < Bsz; ++j) {
          int c = bc + j;
          if (c >= N) break;
          B[c * N + r] = A[r * N + c];
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to handle N not divisible by 32; impact on correctness and performance?
- How would you adapt to double precision or non-square matrices?
- Would you vectorize with AVX/NEON and what changes? How to measure speedup?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical memory management, lock-free data structures, and safe object reuse under contention. A beginner-friendly allocator emphasizes alignment and simple reclaim strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free freelist using CAS
- ABA avoidance (tagged pointers or hazard pointers)
- Slab-style pool with 64-byte blocks
- Cache-line awareness and fragmentation

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code for allocate/free
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;
typedef struct Pool { Block* head; void* pool; size_t block_size; } Pool;

Block* allocate(Pool* p){
  Block* old = atomic_load(&p->head);
  while (old){
    Block* next = old->next;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->head, &old, next)){
      return old;
    }
  }
  return NULL;
}
void deallocate(Pool* p, Block* b){
  Block* head = atomic_load(&p->head);
  do {
    b->next = head;
  } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->head, &head, b));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect pool exhaustion and recover?
- How would you extend to variable-size objects or per-object headers?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about memory access patterns and cache locality in a practical loop, not just algorithmic correctness.

## Key Concepts
- Cache locality
- Blocking/tiling
- L1 data reuse
- Edge-case handling

## Code Example
```javascript
void transpose(int N, int M, const int A[][M], int B[][N]) {
  const int BS = 16;
  for (int r = 0; r < N; r += BS)
    for (int c = 0; c < M; c += BS) {
      int rmax = (r + BS > N) ? N : r + BS;
      int cmax = (c + BS > M) ? M : c + BS;
      for (int i = r; i < rmax; ++i)
        for (int j = c; j < cmax; ++j)
          B[j][i] = A[i][j];
    }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you benchmark the cache miss rate vs a naive row-major transpose?
- How does tile size affect performance on different CPU architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests low-level memory management, determinism, and fragmentation handling in tight constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool vs variable allocations
- In-block free-list approach
- Alignment and fragmentation
- Initialization, allocation, freeing, and testing

## Code Example

```c
/* Initialization and simple allocator skeleton */
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;

static uint8_t pool[4096];
static Block* free_list;

void pool_init(void){
  free_list = (Block*)pool;
  Block* cur = free_list;
  for (size_t i=0; i< (4096/32)-1; ++i){
    cur->next = (Block*)((uint8_t*)cur + 32);
    cur = cur->next;
  }
  cur->next = NULL;
}
void* pool_alloc(size_t s){
  if (s>32) return NULL;
  if (!free_list) return NULL;
  Block* b = free_list;
  free_list = b->next;
  return (void*)b;
}
void pool_free(void* p){
  ((Block*)p)->next = free_list;
  free_list = (Block*)p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you add support for variable-sized allocations with splitting and coalescing?
- How would you verify correctness with a tiny unit test harness on a microcontroller?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of NUMA locality, cache-line effects, and lock-free cross-core pooling. Candidates must design per-core data structures, safe cross-core stealing, and benchmarking plans.

## Key Concepts

- NUMA-aware memory locality
- Cache-line padding to avoid false sharing
- Lock-free freelists and batch stealing
- Cross-socket latency and fragmentation analysis

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct Obj {
  struct Obj *next;
} Obj;
typedef struct Slab {
  Obj *free;
  // cache-line padding to avoid false sharing
  char pad[64 - sizeof(void*)];
} Slab;
```

